// RetryStrategy.java
public interface RetryStrategy {
    void execute(Runnable task);
}

// ExponentialBackoffRetry.java
public class ExponentialBackoffRetry implements RetryStrategy {
    private int maxRetries = 5;
    private int delay = 100; // milliseconds

    @Override
    public void execute(Runnable task) {
        for (int attempt = 0; attempt <= maxRetries; attempt++) {
            try {
                task.run();
                return;
            } catch (Exception e) {
                if (attempt == maxRetries) throw new RuntimeException("Max retries exceeded", e);
                try { Thread.sleep(delay); } catch (InterruptedException ie) {}
                delay *= 2; // Exponential backoff
            }
        }
    }
}

// NotificationManager.java
public class NotificationManager {
    private final List<NotificationService> services;

    public NotificationManager(List<NotificationService> services) {
        this.services = services;
    }

    public void send(String recipient, String subject, String body) {
        for (NotificationService service : services) {
            if (service instanceof EmailNotification) {
                ((EmailNotification) service).sendEmail(recipient, subject, body);
            }
        }
    }

    public void send(String phoneNumber, String message) {
        for (NotificationService service : services) {
            if (service instanceof SMSNotification) {
                ((SMSNotification) service).sendSMS(phoneNumber, message);
            }
        }
    }

    public void send(String userId, String title, String content) {
        for (NotificationService service : services) {
            if (service instanceof PushNotification) {
                ((PushNotification) service).sendPushNotification(userId, title, content);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

This code defines a basic structure for a notification system in Java. It includes interfaces and classes for different types of notifications (email, SMS, push), a retry strategy with exponential backoff, and a manager class to handle the dispatching of notifications across multiple services. The actual implementation of the third-party client classes (`EmailClient`, `SMSClient`, `PushNotificationClient`) would depend on the specific APIs provided by these services.

This design is modular and scalable, allowing for easy addition of new notification types or external services in the future. It also incorporates basic retry mechanisms to handle transient network issues, which is crucial for real-time delivery systems.']

