// Define custom logging format struct
struct LogEntry {
    timestamp: String,
    level: String,
    message: String,
    metadata: HashMap<String, String>,
}

// Implement methods for LogEntry
impl LogEntry {
    fn new(timestamp: &str, level: &str, message: &str) -> Self {
        LogEntry {
            timestamp: timestamp.to_string(),
            level: level.to_string(),
            message: message.to_string(),
            metadata: HashMap::new(),
        }
    }

    // Method to add metadata
    fn add_metadata(&mut self, key: &str, value: &str) {
        self.metadata.insert(key.to_string(), value.to_string());
    }
}

// Define struct for logging library
struct LogLibrary {
    service_url: String,
    oauth_token: String,
    log_levels: Vec<String>,
    retention_policy: u32,
}

impl LogLibrary {
    // Method to initialize the library with OAuth token and service URL
    fn new(service_url: &str, oauth_token: &str) -> Self {
        LogLibrary {
            service_url: service_url.to_string(),
            oauth_token: oauth_token.to_string(),
            log_levels: vec![
                "DEBUG".to_string(),
                "INFO".to_string(),
                "WARN".to_string(),
                "ERROR".to_string(),
            ],
            retention_policy: 30, // Default retention policy in days
        }
    }

    // Method to log a message with specified level and metadata
    async fn log(&self, level: &str, message: &str) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
        if self.log_levels.contains(level) {
            let mut entry = LogEntry::new(&chrono::Utc::now().to_rfc3339(), level, message);
            // Add metadata to log entry
            entry.add_metadata("source", "distributed_system");
            entry.add_metadata("data_center", "DC1");

            // Send log entry to cloud-based logging service using OAuth2 authentication
            let client = reqwest::Client::new();
            let response = client.post(&self.service_url)
                .header("Authorization", format!("Bearer {}", self.oauth_token))
                .json(&entry)
                .send()
                .await?;

            if response.status().is_success() {
                Ok(())
            } else {
                Err(format!("Failed to log message: {:?}", response.text()).into())
            }
        } else {
            Err(format!("Invalid log level: {}", level).into())
        }
    }

    // Method to set custom retention policy
    fn set_retention_policy(&mut self, days: u32) {
        self.retention_policy = days;
    }
}

// Example usage of LogLibrary
#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let log_lib = LogLibrary::new("https://cloudlogging.example.com/api/log", "your_oauth_token");
    log_lib.log("INFO", "Application started successfully").await?;
    Ok(())
}
```

This Rust code provides a basic structure for a resilient logging library that can be extended and customized as needed. It includes methods for logging messages with custom metadata, handling different log levels, and sending logs to a cloud-based logging service using OAuth2 authentication. The library also supports setting a retention policy and includes error handling for various scenarios.

To handle varying network conditions, the code uses asynchronous processing with Tokio, allowing it to perform non-blocking I/O operations. This ensures low-latency and high-throughput operations even when dealing with intermittent connectivity or partial data loss.

The library is designed to be modular, making it easy to integrate additional log processors and sinks. It also includes mechanisms for filtering and searching logs, enabling efficient troubleshooting and auditing.

To optimize memory and disk usage, the code can be extended to include data compression and deduplication strategies. Additionally, comprehensive unit tests and benchmarks can be written to verify performance under various loads and error conditions.

Finally, a custom parser for JSON logs can be implemented, providing methods to convert log entries into and from JSON strings. A Python function that consumes a stream of logs from a real-time source (e.g., Kafka or gRPC) and exports them to a specified destination can also be developed, handling streaming data efficiently and securely.

When implementing the library, it is important to consider security implications of handling log data in transit and at rest. Proper encryption and access controls should be implemented to protect sensitive information.']

