Java function for disjoint pairs using Union-Find:

```java
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class DisjointPairs {

    private static final int[] parent = new int[1000]; // Adjust size based on input constraints

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4};
        long startTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        int count = findDisjointPairs(arr);
        long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        System.out.println("Number of disjoint pairs: " + count);
        System.out.println("Time taken: " + (endTime - startTime) + " ms");
    }

    public static int findDisjointPairs(int[] arr) {
        Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            map.put(arr[i], i);
            union(i, parent);
        }

        int count = 0;
        for (int value : map.values()) {
            if (parent[value] == value) {
                count++;
            }
        }

        return count / 2; // Each disjoint pair contributes to two equal parents
    }

    public static void union(int x, int[] parent) {
        while (parent[x] != x) {
            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]];
            x = parent[x];
        }
    }
}
```

This Java code provides a simple implementation of the Union-Find data structure to find the number of disjoint pairs in an array. The `findDisjointPairs` method uses a map to keep track of the first occurrence of each value, which is then used with the Union-Find algorithm to determine disjointness. Note that the actual disjoint pair count is half of the number of equal parent nodes since each disjoint pair contributes two equal parents.']

